A||||8||Formula 18||Engine Swap wiring||||1||
Z||000000||Formula 18||12-13-2000||08:19 PM||||This seem to be the big hurdle that everyone has when they do engine swaps. I have no clue on the sepifics of how to do it just that you need the wiring sechmatics and both ECMS and harnesses and then piggy back them. <P>I was hoping that some of you might clear up the details. ||||reg||1
Z||000001||terryk||12-14-2000||09:11 PM||||Actually, the wiring is the smallest hurdle. Takes the most time, but it's not a big deal. If you think about it, 98% of the wires go between the engine and ECM. The rest are power, ground, gauges, AC, fuel pumps, fan, etc. One here, two there....<P>I wouldn't piggy back the harnesses, rather, install the donor harness and cut/route/weave it into the car. You will use a few connectors (C203, C100) but not too many others. You might steal some wires to lengthen things.<P>Get the factory manuals for the donor and recipent cars. What you will find it that almost all of the wires do the same thing.<P>Terry<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Formula 18||12-14-2000||11:02 PM||||So I just need the ECM and the harness from the donor car then use a diagram to patch a few wires to the speedo, tach, ect.<BR>I found this on "Dancar Motorsports"  aka Fieroman's website <A HREF="http://www.ipass.net/~fieroman/terranexus//fieroman/ecmschem.gif" TARGET=_blank>http://www.ipass.net/~fieroman/terranexus//fieroman/ecmschem.gif</A> <P>On Archie's site it shows them piggy backing two computers. Is this just for the TPI 350's or is this common?<p>[This message has been edited by Formula 18 (edited 12-14-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||pherder||12-15-2000||07:52 AM||||The ONLY reason I can think of to piggy back ECMs is for the 88 Fiero only.<P>One of the engine's ECM (V6 or L4) had the cruise control in the ECM instead of separate like earlier years.<P>Otherwise you can do away with the Fiero's ECM.  I put a Quad 4 in my 88 Coupe and have the separate control box for the cruise control but I don't know where this control box came from ... I also had an 88 V6 Formula that I stripped for parts that had a cruise control in it and it could of came from either car.<P>Paul||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||12-15-2000||10:36 AM||||If you pick an engine where the ECM controls the cruise, it's not problem. The external cruise controller and ECM use the same servo, switches, etc. Just move them to the ECM. As for the 88, just go backwards. Add the cruise module from an earlier GM car. You will need VSS in (2000 PPM).<P>I'm trying to think of *any* reason to have the stock ECM in the car after the swap. I can't think of one.<P>The speedo is about the only real issue.In the Fiero, the VSS drives the speedo which in turn drives the ECM. On the donor, the VSS will drive the ECM which in turn will drive the speedo. To do this, the output of the ECM should have a pullup resistor of about 2.7k to +12. Then take the output of the ECM through a 10uF cap to the speedo. Depending on the donor, you may have to have the speedo calibrations changed in the ECM or add a speedo box (DRAC).<P>The remaining one is if there is a variable AC pressure sender. This may have to be spliced into the pressure side of the line or screwed onto the high pressure port.<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 12-15-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Richard Parnell||12-15-2000||01:15 PM||||I did a v8 swap on my 87 sc.  I cannot get the speedo to work.  It sits at 20 mph all the time stopped or going on or off.  Is the ere some grounding wire that got cut when the ECM was removed.  You mentioned that the speedo goes to the ecm.  Is there a return voltage it's looking for from the non-existant ecm?  I'm at a loss.  Any help will be appreciated.  Thanks<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000006||Will||12-15-2000||02:47 PM||||Actually, I thought that the speedo would not be that hard... (in most cases)<BR>     Unless you want to use an electronic speed limiter (why the hell would you want to do that?) the only thing that's necessary is that the ECM know the car is moving, so that it can keep from stalling if you sink the clutch at speed. Provided you are using a stock Fiero transmission, just hook the speedo up in the stock fashion and help the pulse train find its way to the ECM (within proper voltage limits, of course). It doesn't matter that the ECM doesn't know the correct speed.<BR>     It only gets complicated if you are using a transmission with the 40(?) PPR VSS, of which the usual specimens are 4T60E,4T65E,4T80E. I guess the 284 Getrag uses the 40 PPR VSS?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Richard Parnell||12-15-2000||03:27 PM||||I do not have the ecm anymore.  The car is carbed V8.  My question above still stands.  Any suggestions. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||terryk||12-15-2000||04:10 PM||||There are two wires in C203 (purple and yellow) that go from the VSS, through C203 to the speedo. Without them, no speedo. When you ripped out the ECM, did you rip out C203. It's a clear dual 8 pin? connector located under the console just in front of the ECM. The VSS wires come up to the engine harness, through the bulkhead connector, past the ECM and down to C203. From there, the wires go along the console to the IP.<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 12-15-2000).]||||reg||1
